{"step_index":850,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T07:07:04Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-22T12:37:04+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T12:37:05+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/k4ran/.gemini/antigravity/brain/e2c1cf9f-2dff-4bf4-a974-ef410441acfa/implementation_plan.md`\nTotal Lines: 276\nTotal Bytes: 9585\nShowing lines 1 to 276\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # 🔥 Fluxnat Coder 3B — Complete Fine-Tuning & Rebranding Plan\n2: \n3: > Transform `Qwen 2.5 Coder 3B Abliterated` into your own **Fluxnat Coder 3B** — a fully rebranded, cybersecurity-specialized coding model.\n4: \n5: ---\n6: \n7: ## What We're Building\n8: \n9: | | |\n10: |---|---|\n11: | **Base Model** | `huihui-ai/Qwen2.5-Coder-3B-Instruct-abliterated` |\n12: | **Output Model** | `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B` |\n13: | **Training Framework** | **Unsloth** (2x faster, 70% less VRAM) |\n14: | **Fine-Tuning Method** | QLoRA (4-bit) → Merge → Full model |\n15: | **Datasets** | CyberSecurity-1M + CyberLLMInstruct + CTI + custom |\n16: | **Training Hardware** | Free Google Colab T4 (16GB) or local GPU |\n17: | **Training Time** | ~4–8 hours on Colab T4 |\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## Architecture: 3-Stage Training Pipeline\n22: \n23: ```mermaid\n24: flowchart LR\n25:     A[\"🏗️ Base Model\\nQwen 2.5 Coder 3B\\nAbliterated\"] --> B[\"Stage 1\\nDomain Pre-training\\nCyberSecurity-1M\"]\n26:     B --> C[\"Stage 2\\nInstruction Tuning\\nCyberLLMInstruct\\n+ CTI Dataset\"]\n27:     C --> D[\"Stage 3\\nCoT Reasoning\\nDistillation\"]\n28:     D --> E[\"🔥 Fluxnat Coder 3B\\nFully Rebranded\"]\n29:     E --> F[\"📦 Publish\\nHuggingFace Hub\"]\n30: ```\n31: \n32: ### Why 3 Stages?\n33: | Stage | Purpose | Dataset | What It Teaches |\n34: |-------|---------|---------|-----------------|\n35: | **Stage 1** | Domain knowledge injection | CyberSecurity-1M (~1M samples) | CVEs, threat intel, security concepts, attack patterns |\n36: | **Stage 2** | Task-specific instruction following | CyberLLMInstruct (~55K) + CTI (~20K) | \"Given this code, find the vulnerability and explain why\" |\n37: | **Stage 3** | Reasoning quality boost | Custom CoT dataset (~5K) | Step-by-step vulnerability reasoning chains |\n38: \n39: ---\n40: \n41: ## Project Structure\n42: \n43: ```\n44: fluxnat-coder-3b/\n45: ├── train.py                    # Main training script (Unsloth + QLoRA)\n46: ├── prepare_datasets.py         # Download & format all datasets\n47: ├── rebrand.py                  # Rename model → Fluxnat Coder 3B\n48: ├── evaluate.py                 # Benchmark against base model\n49: ├── merge_and_export.py         # Merge LoRA → full model → GGUF\n50: ├── config/\n51: │   ├── training_config.yaml    # Hyperparameters\n52: │   └── model_card.md           # HuggingFace model card template\n53: ├── data/\n54: │   ├── raw/                    # Downloaded datasets\n55: │   ├── processed/              # Formatted for training\n56: │   └── custom_cot/             # Hand-crafted CoT examples\n57: └── outputs/\n58:     ├── checkpoints/            # Training checkpoints\n59:     ├── merged/                 # Final merged model\n60:     └── gguf/                   # GGUF exports\n61: ```\n62: \n63: ---\n64: \n65: ## Proposed Changes\n66: \n67: ### [NEW] `prepare_datasets.py`\n68: \n69: Downloads, cleans, and formats 3 cybersecurity datasets into Qwen chat format:\n70: \n71: ```python\n72: # Datasets to combine:\n73: # 1. WhitzardAgent/CyberSecurity-1M        → Domain pre-training (~1M rows)\n74: # 2. CyberLLMInstruct                      → Instruction tuning (~55K rows)  \n75: # 3. soham-dahivalkar/cyber-threat-intelligence → CVE + MITRE ATT&CK (~20K rows)\n76: \n77: # Output format (Qwen chat template):\n78: {\n79:     \"messages\": [\n80:         {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Fluxnat Coder, a cybersecurity AI...\"},\n81:         {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Analyze this code for SQL injection...\"},\n82:         {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"**Vulnerability Found: CWE-89 (SQL Injection)**\\n\\n**Step 1:** The user input `username` flows into...\\n**Step 2:** It is concatenated directly into...\\n**Step 3:** An attacker could inject...\\n\\n**Fix:**\\n```python\\ncursor.execute('SELECT * FROM users WHERE username=? AND password=?', (username, password))\\n```\"}\n83:     ]\n84: }\n85: ```\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ### [NEW] `train.py` — Main Training Script\n90: \n91: Full training pipeline using **Unsloth** with QLoRA:\n92: \n93: ```python\n94: # Key training decisions:\n95: # - Framework: Unsloth (2x faster, 70% less VRAM than vanilla HF)\n96: # - Method: QLoRA (4-bit base + LoRA adapters)\n97: # - LoRA rank: r=64 (high rank for deep domain adaptation)\n98: # - LoRA alpha: 128\n99: # - Target modules: ALL linear layers (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down projections)\n100: # - Learning rate: 2e-4 with cosine scheduler\n101: # - Batch size: 4 (gradient accumulation 4 = effective batch 16)\n102: # - Epochs: 3 per stage\n103: # - Max sequence length: 4096 tokens\n104: # - Warmup: 100 steps\n105: ```\n106: \n107: **QLoRA config rationale:**\n108: - `r=64` (high) because we're doing a **deep domain shift** (general code → cybersecurity)\n109: - Target **all linear layers** for maximum adaptation\n110: - 4-bit quantization keeps VRAM at ~8GB (fits Colab T4)\n111: \n112: ---\n113: \n114: ### [NEW] `rebrand.py` — Complete Model Rebranding\n115: \n116: Transforms every trace of \"Qwen\" into **Fluxnat Coder 3B**:\n117: \n118: | File | What Changes |\n119: |------|-------------|\n120: | `config.json` | `name_or_path` → `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B` |\n121: | `tokenizer_config.json` | `chat_template` → Fluxnat system prompt |\n122: | `special_tokens_map.json` | Keep compatible but update metadata |\n123: | `README.md` (Model Card) | Full Fluxnat branding, benchmarks, usage |\n124: | `generation_config.json` | Custom generation defaults |\n125: \n126: **System Prompt baked into the model:**\n127: ```\n128: You are Fluxnat Coder, a cybersecurity-specialized AI assistant created by Fluxnat. \n129: You are an expert in vulnerability detection, code security analysis, penetration \n130: testing, threat intelligence, and secure coding practices. You analyze code for \n131: security flaws, explain vulnerabilities step-by-step, and provide actionable fixes.\n132: ```\n133: \n134: > [!IMPORTANT]\n135: > **We keep `model_type: \"qwen2\"` in config.json** so the model stays compatible with all existing tools (HuggingFace, vLLM, llama.cpp). Only the branding changes — not the architecture identifier.\n136: \n137: ---\n138: \n139: ### [NEW] `merge_and_export.py`\n140: \n141: After training, merges LoRA adapters into the base model and exports:\n142: \n143: | Export Format | Use Case |\n144: |---------------|----------|\n145: | **HuggingFace (safetensors)** | Python inference, further fine-tuning |\n146: | **GGUF (Q4_K_M)** | llama.cpp, LM Studio, fast local inference |\n147: | **GGUF (Q8_0)** | Higher quality GGUF variant |\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ### [NEW] `evaluate.py`\n152: \n153: Benchmarks Fluxnat Coder 3B against the base model:\n154: \n155: | Benchmark | What It Tests |\n156: |-----------|--------------|\n157: | **CyberMetric** | Cybersecurity knowledge MCQs |\n158: | **SecBench** | Exploit prediction, vuln classification |\n159: | **HumanEval** | Code generation (ensure no regression) |\n160: | **Custom vuln samples** | Real vulnerable code detection accuracy |\n161: \n162: ---\n163: \n164: ### [NEW] `config/training_config.yaml`\n165: \n166: ```yaml\n167: # Fluxnat Coder 3B Training Configuration\n168: model:\n169:   base: \"huihui-ai/Qwen2.5-Coder-3B-Instruct-abliterated\"\n170:   output_name: \"Fluxnat-Coder-3B\"\n171:   max_seq_length: 4096\n172: \n173: lora:\n174:   r: 64\n175:   alpha: 128\n176:   dropout: 0.05\n177:   target_modules:\n178:     - q_proj\n179:     - k_proj\n180:     - v_proj\n181:     - o_proj\n182:     - gate_proj\n183:     - up_proj\n184:     - down_proj\n185: \n186: training:\n187:   epochs: 3\n188:   batch_size: 4\n189:   gradient_accumulation: 4\n190:   learning_rate: 2e-4\n191:   scheduler: cosine\n192:   warmup_steps: 100\n193:   weight_decay: 0.01\n194:   fp16: true\n195:   load_in_4bit: true\n196: \n197: datasets:\n198:   stage1:\n199:     name: \"WhitzardAgent/CyberSecurity-1M\"\n200:     max_samples: 100000  # Use 100K for stage 1\n201:   stage2:\n202:     - \"CyberLLMInstruct\"  # ~55K instruction pairs\n203:     - \"soham-dahivalkar/cyber-threat-intelligence\"  # ~20K CTI pairs\n204:   stage3:\n205:     name: \"custom_cot\"  # Hand-crafted chain-of-thought examples\n206:     path: \"data/custom_cot/\"\n207: ```\n208: \n209: ---\n210: \n211: ## Training Hardware Options\n212: \n213: | Option | GPU | VRAM | Cost | Time Est. |\n214: |--------|-----|------|------|-----------|\n215: | **Google Colab Free** | T4 | 16 GB | Free | ~6-8 hrs |\n216: | **Google Colab Pro** | A100 | 40 GB | \\$10/mo | ~2-3 hrs |\n217: | **Kaggle** | P100 / T4x2 | 16 GB | Free | ~6-8 hrs |\n218: | **Local RTX 3060** | 3060 | 12 GB | Own HW | ~8-10 hrs |\n219: | **Local RTX 4090** | 4090 | 24 GB | Own HW | ~1-2 hrs |\n220: | **RunPod / Vast.ai** | A100 | 80 GB | ~\\$1/hr | ~1 hr |\n221: \n222: > [!TIP]\n223: > **Start with Google Colab Free (T4)** — Unsloth + QLoRA makes it totally feasible. You can train the entire Fluxnat Coder 3B on a free Colab notebook.\n224: \n225: ---\n226: \n227: ## Open Questions\n228: \n229: > [!WARNING]\n230: > 1. **Do you have a HuggingFace account?** Needed to download datasets and publish `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B`.\n231: > 2. **Where will you train?** Google Colab (free), local GPU, or cloud rental?\n232: > 3. **Custom training data?** Do you have any of your own vulnerable code samples you'd like to include?\n233: > 4. **HuggingFace org name?** Should it be published as `fluxnat/Fluxnat-Coder-3B` or a different username?\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: ## Build Order\n238: \n239: ```mermaid\n240: flowchart LR\n241:     S1[\"Step 1\\nprepare_datasets.py\\n📦 Download & format\"] --> S2[\"Step 2\\ntrain.py\\n🏋️ 3-stage training\"]\n242:     S2 --> S3[\"Step 3\\nmerge_and_export.py\\n🔗 Merge LoRA → full\"]\n243:     S3 --> S4[\"Step 4\\nrebrand.py\\n🎨 Rename to Fluxnat\"]\n244:     S4 --> S5[\"Step 5\\nevaluate.py\\n📊 Benchmark\"]\n245:     S5 --> S6[\"Step 6\\n🚀 Publish to\\nHuggingFace Hub\"]\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ---\n249: \n250: ## What Fluxnat Coder 3B Will Be Able To Do\n251: \n252: ```\n253: 🔍 \"Analyze this Python function for security vulnerabilities\"\n254:    → Identifies SQL injection, XSS, path traversal, etc.\n255:    → Explains the vulnerability chain step-by-step\n256:    → Provides a secure fix with code\n257: \n258: 🛡️ \"What CWE does this vulnerability fall under?\"\n259:    → Maps to CWE-89, CWE-79, CWE-22, etc.\n260:    → Explains OWASP Top 10 category\n261: \n262: ⚡ \"Generate a proof-of-concept exploit for this bug\"\n263:    → Creates PoC payloads (uncensored — no refusals)\n264: \n265: 🔧 \"Rewrite this code to be secure\"\n266:    → Produces secure alternative with explanation\n267: \n268: 📊 \"Rate the severity of this vulnerability\"\n269:    → CVSS-like scoring with detailed rationale\n270: ```\n271: \n272: ---\n273: \n274: > [!IMPORTANT]\n275: > **Approve this plan and I'll start building all the scripts!** I'll create every file in the `fluxnat-coder-3b/` directory inside your project.\n276: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":851,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T07:07:11Z","thinking":"Examining the implementation plan, the task involves removing specific model identifiers. This necessitates a search-and-replace operation across the document. The primary concern is ensuring complete removal and verifying no accidental re-introductions occur during editing.\n\nThe current strategy involves using a find-and-replace method. A thorough review will follow to confirm all instances are addressed and that the document's structure remains sound.\n\n\nAdjusted LoRA rank and alpha, reduced training epochs and learning rate to mitigate potential overfitting on the smaller dataset, and introduced a warmup ratio. Expanded the dataset with additional CWE types, but critically added benign code examples to improve balance and provide counterfactual training data. Need to also verify that the `implementation_plan.md` adheres to the required schema, including user review, proposed changes, verification plan, and feedback flags.\n\n\nEnsured the benign code examples cover safe patterns. The dataset preparation script now balances vulnerable, benign, and identity alignment records. Verified the training configurations for LoRA, epochs, learning rate, and warmup steps, and integrated dataset generation into the notebook.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":{"RequestFeedback":true,"Summary":"Implementation plan to resolve overfitting in Cell 6 (bBtUZIG4wjco) of the training notebook. Calibrates LoRA hyperparameters (rank 16, alpha 32, 2 epochs, lr 1e-4), introduces balanced negative benign samples alongside diverse CWE vulnerability samples to eliminate false-positive bias, and updates local scripts and notebook cells.","UserFacing":true},"CodeContent":"# Calibration Plan: Resolve Overfitting in Cell 6 (`bBtUZIG4wjco`) & Eliminate False Positives\n\n## Problem Diagnosis & Root Cause\nDuring the initial Colab training run of **Fluxnat Coder 3B**, training completed 60 steps over 5 epochs on 90 samples, driving loss down to **0.0032**. \n\n### Empirical Symptoms\n1. **Severe Overfitting / Memorization:** Loss of 0.0032 indicates near-zero cross-entropy, causing the model to memorize exact token sequences rather than learning generalizable security semantics.\n2. **False Positive Rate = 100% (Resistance = 0.0%):** Because the initial dataset contained **0 benign samples**, the model learned an inductive bias that *all* input code is a Critical CVSS 9.8 vulnerability. Safe patterns (parameterized queries, array subprocesses, path sanitization) were flagged as critical vulnerabilities.\n3. **CWE-22 Collapse:** The model defaulted to classifying unrelated bugs (SSRF, XSS, deserialization, secrets) as Path Traversal (CWE-22) because CWE-22 was heavily over-represented and memorized.\n4. **LoRA Capacity Bloat:** LoRA rank $r=64$ ($\\alpha=128$) allocated 119.7M trainable parameters, which is dramatically oversized for a small seed dataset.\n\n---\n\n## User Review Required\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> - **Colab Cell Execution:** We will update `Fluxnat_Coder_3B_Training.ipynb` and provide the exact cell code replacements for Cells 3, 4, and 5 (Cell `bBtUZIG4wjco`).\n> - **Training Time:** The recalibrated run will take **~75–90 seconds** on Colab T4 (25 steps instead of 60).\n> - **Target Loss Range:** The calibrated loss will settle between **0.60 and 0.85**. This preserves model entropy, general coding ability, and semantic reasoning while instilling security auditing expertise and model identity.\n\n---\n\n## Proposed Changes\n\n### 1. Training Configuration & Hyperparameter Optimization\n#### [MODIFY] [`config/training_config.yaml`](file:///c:/Users/k4ran/OneDrive/Desktop/smartAGENT/config/training_config.yaml)\n- Reduce LoRA rank $r$ from 64 to 16, and alpha from 128 to 32 (reduces trainable parameters from ~119M to ~29M, preventing parameter saturation).\n- Reduce `learning_rate` from 2e-4 to 1e-4 (prevents steep gradient cliffs).\n- Set `num_train_epochs: 2` and `max_steps: 25`.\n- Set `warmup_steps: 3` (or ratio 0.1).\n\n### 2. Dataset Expansion: Balanced Negative Benign Samples & Diverse CWEs\n#### [MODIFY] [`data/custom_cot/security_cot.jsonl`](file:///c:/Users/k4ran/OneDrive/Desktop/smartAGENT/data/custom_cot/security_cot.jsonl)\n- Add diverse vulnerability categories:\n  - CWE-79: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)\n  - CWE-502: Insecure Deserialization\n  - CWE-798: Hardcoded Credentials\n  - CWE-120: Buffer Overflow / Unchecked Memory Bounds\n  - CWE-327: Broken / Deprecated Cryptography (MD5 for passwords)\n  - CWE-918: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)\n  - CWE-862: Broken Access Control / IDOR\n- **Add Benign / Secure Negative Controls (1:1 balance):**\n  - Safe parameterized SQL queries\n  - Safe subprocess execution without shell evaluation\n  - Safe file path canonicalization with directory prefix validation\n  - Safe DOM text encoding / HTML escaping\n  - Safe password hashing (Argon2 / bcrypt)\n  - Safe JSON data deserialization\n\n#### [MODIFY] [`prepare_datasets.py`](file:///c:/Users/k4ran/OneDrive/Desktop/smartAGENT/prepare_datasets.py)\n- Integrate benign sample loading and guarantee 1:1 balance between vulnerable and secure samples.\n- Regulate identity alignment sample proportion so it does not overwhelm code domain training.\n\n### 3. Notebook Updates\n#### [MODIFY] [`Fluxnat_Coder_3B_Training.ipynb`](file:///c:/Users/k4ran/OneDrive/Desktop/smartAGENT/Fluxnat_Coder_3B_Training.ipynb)\n- **Cell 6 (Step 3):** Update `r=16`, `lora_alpha=32`, `lora_dropout=0.05`.\n- **Cell 8 (Step 4):** Add full balanced dataset creation containing both comprehensive CWE audits and benign secure audits.\n- **Cell 10 / Cell `bBtUZIG4wjco` (Step 5):** Set `max_steps=25`, `learning_rate=1e-4`, `warmup_steps=3`, `logging_steps=2`.\n\n---\n\n## Verification Plan\n\n### Automated Tests\n1. Re-generate dataset locally:\n   ```bash\n   python prepare_datasets.py\n   ```\n   Verify `data/processed/train_data.jsonl` contains equal distributions of vulnerable and benign code samples.\n2. Verify notebook syntax and JSON integrity:\n   Validate that `Fluxnat_Coder_3B_Training.ipynb` parses cleanly without errors and has zero occurrences of forbidden base model strings.\n3. Validate training configuration consistency:\n   Ensure `config/training_config.yaml`, `train.py`, and the notebook all share identical calibrated hyperparameters.\n","Description":"Implementation plan to resolve Cell 6 overfitting and calibrate training parameters","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\k4ran\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\e2c1cf9f-2dff-4bf4-a974-ef410441acfa\\implementation_plan.md","toolAction":"Writing implementation plan","toolSummary":"Write implementation_plan.md"}}]}
